{-
   Constant folding across the integer/floating boundary, and the two places it declines.

   `.opt.expect` is the assertion, because what folding does is leave nothing behind: a function here
   either returns one number by the time the optimizer is finished or it still contains the operation
   this pass would not perform. The run value is asserted on both targets and against the unoptimized
   build for the usual reason - a fold that picked the wrong answer is only visible as a number.
-}

-- Every direction of conversion, all of them exact. `2` reaches `wideningInt` as an `Int` and leaves
-- as a `Double`; `narrowing` rounds a double to a float and back; `truncating` cuts toward zero,
-- which for a negative value means *up* and is the half of the rule an implementation gets wrong.
fn wideningInt() -> Double = 7 :: Double

fn narrowing() -> Float = truncate((2.5 :: Double)) :: Float

fn widening() -> Double = (2.5 :: Float) :: Double

fn truncating() -> Int = truncate((9.75 :: Double)) :: Int

fn truncatingNegative() -> Int = truncate((-9.75 :: Double)) :: Int

-- Arithmetic first, conversion second, and the whole chain is one number. `Float` is the interesting
-- width: natively this is single-precision throughout and on JS it is a double rounded by
-- `Math.fround` at each step, and the two agree because rounding a double result to single is the
-- same value as computing in single.
fn arithmetic() -> Int = truncate(((1.5 :: Float) * 4.0 + 3.0 - 1.0)) :: Int

fn divided() -> Int = truncate(((45.0 :: Double) / 4.0)) :: Int

-- IEEE ordering over two constants, which is specified exactly and identical on both targets.
fn compared() -> Int = if (2.5 :: Double) > 2.0 then 30 else 0

{-
   Out of range, which used to be the first decline and is now a value.

   Truncating `1e30` is not a number any `Int` has, and until this was ruled on it was exactly where
   the two targets stopped agreeing - natively the conversion produced the integer indefinite value,
   while `codegen/js` emitted `Math.trunc` and then the coercion the type needs, which is modular.
   Folding it would have been this pass choosing which of them the language meant.

   The ruling is saturation, so out of range has one answer on every target and this folds to it. The
   two ends are asserted separately because they are not one case in any implementation of the rule:
   on x86 the hardware already produces the low one and only the high one is a fixup.
-}
fn outOfRange() -> Int = truncate((1e30 :: Double)) :: Int

fn outOfRangeLow() -> Int = truncate((-1e30 :: Double)) :: Int

fn saturatedConstants() -> Int =
    if outOfRange() == 2147483647 && outOfRangeLow() < 0 - 2147483647 then 40 else 0

{-
   And the same rule where the folder cannot see it, which is the half that tests the three backends
   rather than this pass.

   Both of these produce a value no backend has a literal for - that is what keeps them out of the
   folder, and it is the same reason `nonFinite` below stays - so what reaches the conversion is a
   run-time NaN and a run-time infinity. The rule says zero for the first and the maximum for the
   second, and the two targets have to say so alike.
-}
@noinline
fn notANumber() -> Double = 0.0 / 0.0

@noinline
fn infinite() -> Double = 1.0 / 0.0

fn saturatedAtRuntime() -> Int =
    if (truncate(notANumber()) :: Int) == 0 && (truncate(infinite()) :: Int) == 2147483647 then 50 else 0

{-
   The second decline: a result neither emitter has a literal for.

   `1 / 0` is an infinity rather than an error in floating arithmetic, and both targets produce one -
   but a folded constant has to be *written*, and `Infinity` is not something either backend emits.
   So the division stays and the infinity is produced at run time, where both agree about it.
-}
fn nonFinite() -> Int = if (1.0 :: Double) / 0.0 > 1000000.0 then 20 else 0

fn main() -> Int =
    (truncate(wideningInt()) :: Int) +
    (truncate(narrowing()) :: Int) +
    (truncate(widening()) :: Int) +
    truncating() +
    truncatingNegative() +
    arithmetic() +
    divided() +
    compared() +
    saturatedConstants() +
    saturatedAtRuntime() +
    nonFinite()
